<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">1662533</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">1977861</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Uptake of 11C-methionine in breast cancer studied by PET. An association with the size of S-phase fraction.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Leskinen-Kallio</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>N&#x000e5;gren</surname><given-names>K.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lehikoinen</surname><given-names>P.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ruotsalainen</surname><given-names>U.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Joensuu</surname><given-names>H.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>Department of Oncology and Radiotherapy, Turku University Central Hospital, Finland.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>12</month><year>1991</year></pub-date><volume>64</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>1121</fpage><lpage>1124</lpage><abstract><p>L-[methyl-11-C]methionine (11C-methionine) uptake of seven primary breast cancers, four soft tissue metastases of breast cancer, and three other breast lesions was studied by positron emission tomography (PET). 11C-methionine accumulation was assessed by calculating the standardised uptake value (SUV). The mean SUV for breast cancer was 8.5 +/- 3.3 (s.d.), while the maximal uptake in the liver was 12.4 +/- 1.6, in the bone marrow 5.8 +/- 0.7, and in the myocardium 3.4 +/- 0.6. All eight malignant tumours larger than 30 mm in diameter accumulated clearly 11C-methionine, whereas none of the three smaller cancers (from 12 to 15 mm in diameter) were visualised. Strong uptake of 11C-methionine was associated with a large S-phase fraction (SPF) measured with flow cytometry (r = 0.77, P = 0.01), and the non-visualised cancers had all a small SPF (less than 5.5%). One benign tumour (an abscess) accumulated slightly 11C-methionine. The results indicate that both primary and metastatic breast cancer can be effectively imaged with 11C-methionine by PET, and that the accumulation of 11C-methionine may correlate with the proliferation rate of breast carcinoma.</p><sec sec-type="scanned-figures"><title>Images</title><fig id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00076-0149-a" xlink:role="1123"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00076-0149-b" xlink:role="1123"/></fig></sec></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00076-0147.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="1121" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00076-0148.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="1122" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00076-0149.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="1123" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00076-0150.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="1124" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>